<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-linear-systems" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Systems of Linear Equations</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Linear algebra begins with one concrete problem: given several linear equations in
      several unknowns, <em>find every assignment of values to the unknowns that satisfies
      all of the equations at once</em>. A system of linear equations may have exactly one
      solution, no solutions at all, or infinitely many. Which of these occurs, and how to
      describe the solutions when there are infinitely many, is the subject of this
      chapter.
    </p>
    <p>
      The plan is as follows. <xref ref="sec-systems-of-linear-equations" text="type-global"/>
      fixes the vocabulary and shows what solution sets can look like.
      <xref ref="sec-elementary-operations" text="type-global"/> identifies the three
      operations we are allowed to perform on a system without changing its solutions.
      <xref ref="sec-augmented-matrix" text="type-global"/> strips a system down to the
      numbers that matter <mdash /> the augmented matrix <mdash /> and describes the simple
      <q>echelon</q> shapes from which solutions can be read off.
      <xref ref="sec-row-reduction" text="type-global"/> assembles these pieces into a single
      mechanical procedure, the row reduction algorithm, that settles every case. Finally,
      <xref ref="sec-polynomial-interpolation" text="type-global"/> shows the algorithm at
      work on a problem that does not look linear at first sight: fitting a polynomial
      exactly through a set of data points.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="linear-systems/sec-systems-of-linear-equations.ptx" />
